<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2013a" Name="Ubiquinol-6 biosynthesis from 4-hydroxybenzoate" Version="WP287_r20261010185255" Author="[M.Braymer, MaintBot, Ddigles, Egonw, Eweitz, Khanspers]" Last-Modified="20261010185255" Organism="Saccharomyces cerevisiae">
  <Comment Source="WikiPathways-description">The main ubiquinone in Saccharomyces cerevisiae and other Saccharomycetes species is ubiquinone-6. Biosynthesis of 4-hydroxybenzoate, one of the precursors, can occur in yeast by two different paths - it may be produced directly from chorismate by the chorismate pyruvate-lyase reaction (similar to the common pathway in prokaryotes), or it can be synthesized from L-tyrosine, similar to higher eukaryotes (see 4-hydroxybenzoate biosynthesis I (eukaryotes)). At least 10 genes (COQ1-COQ10) have been shown to be required for synthesis of ubiquinone in Saccharomyces cerevisiae.

At least six of these proteins (COQ3, COQ4, COQ5, COQ6, COQ7 and COQ9) were shown to form a complex, which may be attached to the mitochindria inner membrane by the product of COQ2, an integral membrane protein.

Description adapted [yeastgenome.org](from https://pathway.yeastgenome.org/).</Comment>
